<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Metagenes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Metagenes was a Cretan architect of the sixth century BCE, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chersiphron">Chersiphron</a> of Knossos, with whom he built the archaic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-artemis">Temple of Artemis at Ephesus</a>. According to Vitruvius he devised a way of moving the temple's heavy architrave blocks by setting them inside large wooden wheels drawn by oxen, and he wrote with his father a treatise on the temple.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/metagenes.jpg" alt="Metagenes" width="320" height="569" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The devious diesel · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Metagenes1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
